    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That looks like a great start, very professional. Can you expand on the 'Breaks and Movement' section? Maybe add some specific examples of stretches people can do at their desks, and maybe a note on how persistent discomfort (like that nagging ache in your wrist or neck) shouldn't be ignored?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subject: Ensuring a Healthy Workstation for All Our Remote Employees\n\nDear Team,\n\nAs we continue to navigate the challenges of remote work, it's crucial to maintain a healthy and productive work environment. To help prevent repetitive strain injuries (RSI) and promote overall well-being, I would like to share some key points regarding proper workstation ergonomics.\n\n1. Chair and Desk: Ensure your chair supports your lower back and allows your feet to rest flat on the floor. Your desk should be at a height that keeps your wrists straight while typing. Consider using a footrest if needed.\n\n2. Monitor Placement: Position your monitor at eye level to avoid neck strain. The top of the screen should be at or slightly below eye level, and the screen should be about an arm's length away.\n\n3. Keyboard and Mouse: Use a keyboard and mouse that fit comfortably in your hands. Keep your wrists in a neutral position while typing or using the mouse. Consider using ergonomic accessories like wrist rests or a vertical mouse.\n\n4. Breaks and Movement: Take regular breaks to stretch and move around. A short walk or stretching session every hour can help reduce the risk of RSI and improve overall productivity. Here are a few desk-friendly stretches you can do:",
